1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,627
Tonight, a second sun rises over Earth.

2
00:00:02,627 --> 00:00:06,861
It's tiny, blood red, and bright enough to cast shadows at midnight.

3
00:00:06,861 --> 00:00:09,413
That's Jupiter, if Jupiter became a star.

4
00:00:09,413 --> 00:00:12,588
To make it happen, we'd need eighty times its mass.

5
00:00:12,588 --> 00:00:14,020
And the strangest part?

6
00:00:14,020 --> 00:00:16,946
After all that, it would barely get any bigger.

7
00:00:17,646 --> 00:00:20,373
So today, we build that star like a recipe.

8
00:00:20,373 --> 00:00:21,007
Six steps.

9
00:00:21,007 --> 00:00:28,109
We turn up the mass, watch what changes, and find out what happens to Earth, to the Sun, and to Jupiter's moons.

10
00:00:28,109 --> 00:00:29,758
Some of it works out fine.

11
00:00:29,758 --> 00:00:31,406
Some of it really doesn't.

12
00:00:32,106 --> 00:00:33,856
What If Jupiter Became a Star?

13
00:00:34,556 --> 00:00:35,105
Step one.

14
00:00:35,105 --> 00:00:36,386
Know your ingredient.

15
00:00:36,386 --> 00:00:43,097
Jupiter is three hundred eighteen times the mass of Earth, more than twice all the other planets put together.

16
00:00:43,097 --> 00:00:47,185
It's made mostly of hydrogen and helium, the same stuff as the Sun.

17
00:00:47,185 --> 00:00:49,809
That's why it's often called a failed star.

18
00:00:50,509 --> 00:00:52,210
But that nickname is wrong.

19
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:54,856
A star isn't defined by what it's made of.

20
00:00:54,856 --> 00:00:57,439
It's defined by what happens in its core.

21
00:00:57,439 --> 00:01:04,746
Gravity has to squeeze the center so hard that hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium, and that takes millions of degrees.

22
00:01:04,746 --> 00:01:09,408
Jupiter's core is estimated at about twenty four thousand degrees Celsius.

23
00:01:09,408 --> 00:01:09,660
Hot.

24
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:10,794
But nowhere close.

25
00:01:10,794 --> 00:01:13,314
Jupiter never came near to being a star.

26
00:01:14,014 --> 00:01:16,464
Jupiter does glow a little on its own.

27
00:01:16,464 --> 00:01:21,685
It gives off about one point seven times more heat than it receives from the Sun.

28
00:01:21,685 --> 00:01:27,422
That's leftover heat from its formation, leaking out as the planet very slowly contracts.

29
00:01:27,422 --> 00:01:30,065
But a warm glow in infrared isn't fusion.

30
00:01:30,065 --> 00:01:31,934
A cooling ember isn't a fire.

31
00:01:32,634 --> 00:01:33,219
Step two.

32
00:01:33,219 --> 00:01:33,804
Add mass.

33
00:01:33,804 --> 00:01:34,584
A lot of it.

34
00:01:34,584 --> 00:01:36,533
Pile on Jupiter after Jupiter.

35
00:01:36,533 --> 00:01:40,172
And here's the first surprise: Jupiter doesn't swell up.

36
00:01:40,172 --> 00:01:44,136
Its own gravity crushes the new material tighter and tighter.

37
00:01:44,136 --> 00:01:46,736
Add a few Jupiters, and it barely grows.

38
00:01:46,736 --> 00:01:49,530
Add more, and it actually starts to shrink.

39
00:01:50,230 --> 00:01:57,824
Somewhere around thirteen Jupiter masses, the core gets hot enough to fuse deuterium, a rare, heavy form of hydrogen.

40
00:01:57,824 --> 00:02:01,069
It glows for a while, then the deuterium runs out.

41
00:02:01,069 --> 00:02:02,562
Now it's a brown dwarf.

42
00:02:02,562 --> 00:02:04,055
Too big to be a planet.

43
00:02:04,055 --> 00:02:05,548
Too small to be a star.

44
00:02:06,248 --> 00:02:08,176
Brown dwarfs aren't just theory.

45
00:02:08,176 --> 00:02:17,880
The closest ones we know are a pair called Luhman sixteen, found in twenty thirteen with NASA's WISE space telescope, only about six and a half light years away.

46
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:25,896
They're so dim that they hid in plain sight, right next door to the Sun, until an infrared telescope finally caught their faint glow.

47
00:02:26,596 --> 00:02:27,291
Keep going.

48
00:02:27,291 --> 00:02:36,205
At around seventy five to eighty Jupiter masses, the core finally gets hot enough for ordinary hydrogen to fuse, and to keep fusing for good.

49
00:02:36,205 --> 00:02:37,216
That's the line.

50
00:02:37,216 --> 00:02:42,148
Cross it, and Jupiter becomes a red dwarf, the smallest kind of star there is.

51
00:02:42,848 --> 00:02:43,688
And its size?

52
00:02:43,688 --> 00:02:45,562
Smaller than when we started.

53
00:02:45,562 --> 00:02:53,448
In twenty seventeen, astronomers at the University of Cambridge measured one of the smallest stars known, EBLM J0555-57Ab.

54
00:02:53,448 --> 00:02:58,555
It packs about eighty five Jupiter masses into a ball about the size of Saturn.

55
00:02:58,555 --> 00:03:06,053
It circles a Sun like star once every eight days, and it was found when it passed in front of it, dimming its light.

56
00:03:06,053 --> 00:03:08,768
A real star that would fit inside Jupiter.

57
00:03:09,468 --> 00:03:10,128
Step three.

58
00:03:10,128 --> 00:03:11,388
Find the ingredients.

59
00:03:11,388 --> 00:03:13,968
Where do we get seventy nine more Jupiters?

60
00:03:13,968 --> 00:03:14,508
Not here.

61
00:03:14,508 --> 00:03:21,589
Add up Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, every rocky planet, every moon and asteroid, and it comes to less than half a Jupiter.

62
00:03:21,589 --> 00:03:25,610
The rest would have to be brought in from outside the solar system.

63
00:03:25,610 --> 00:03:28,070
It's impossible, but let's say we did it.

64
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:29,360
Step four.

65
00:03:29,360 --> 00:03:29,831
Look up.

66
00:03:29,831 --> 00:03:36,552
A star this small gives off only a few ten thousandths of the Sun's power, and most of that is invisible infrared.

67
00:03:36,552 --> 00:03:40,973
Even at its closest, it's more than six hundred million kilometers from us.

68
00:03:40,973 --> 00:03:46,809
By our rough calculation, it would shine about as bright as the full Moon, maybe a little brighter.

69
00:03:46,809 --> 00:03:51,938
But all of that light would come from a single red point, far too small to show a disc.

70
00:03:52,638 --> 00:03:58,179
For part of every year, it would hang in our night sky, washing the land in a dim red light.

71
00:03:58,179 --> 00:04:02,877
For other months, it would rise and set with the Sun, almost lost in daylight.

72
00:04:02,877 --> 00:04:03,661
And the heat?

73
00:04:03,661 --> 00:04:04,684
Practically none.

74
00:04:04,684 --> 00:04:09,202
By our estimate, it would warm Earth by less than a thousandth of a degree.

75
00:04:09,202 --> 00:04:10,226
No second summer.

76
00:04:10,226 --> 00:04:11,732
Just a strange new light.

77
00:04:12,431 --> 00:04:13,039
Step five.

78
00:04:13,039 --> 00:04:13,890
Watch the Sun.

79
00:04:13,890 --> 00:04:16,443
Gravity notices far more than our eyes do.

80
00:04:16,443 --> 00:04:22,643
Right now, the Sun and Jupiter circle a shared balance point that sits just outside the Sun's surface.

81
00:04:22,643 --> 00:04:31,457
Make Jupiter eighty times heavier, and that point moves out to about zero point three seven astronomical units, almost as far as Mercury's orbit.

82
00:04:31,457 --> 00:04:37,839
The Sun itself would swing around a loop nearly as big as Mercury's orbit, every eleven and a half years.

83
00:04:38,539 --> 00:04:43,205
Seen from another star, we'd no longer look like a single Sun with planets.

84
00:04:43,205 --> 00:04:45,009
We'd be a binary star system.

85
00:04:45,009 --> 00:04:46,875
And that's not unusual at all.

86
00:04:46,875 --> 00:04:53,655
Surveys of nearby Sun like stars find that about forty four percent of them have at least one companion star.

87
00:04:53,655 --> 00:04:55,521
We'd just be joining the club.

88
00:04:56,221 --> 00:05:00,698
That wobble is exactly how astronomers find planets around other stars.

89
00:05:00,698 --> 00:05:03,598
They watch for stars that rock back and forth.

90
00:05:03,598 --> 00:05:07,507
Today, Jupiter tugs the Sun at about twelve meters per second.

91
00:05:07,507 --> 00:05:14,128
With eighty times the mass, by our calculation, the Sun would swing at close to one kilometer per second.

92
00:05:14,128 --> 00:05:17,533
Alien astronomers could spot it from light years away.

93
00:05:18,233 --> 00:05:18,832
Step six.

94
00:05:18,832 --> 00:05:20,030
Check the planets.

95
00:05:20,030 --> 00:05:24,823
A second star five point two astronomical units out reshapes everything.

96
00:05:24,823 --> 00:05:35,939
Using a standard stability rule for planets in binary star systems, our rough calculation says orbits can stay stable out to about two astronomical units from the Sun.

97
00:05:35,939 --> 00:05:39,134
Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars could keep going.

98
00:05:39,134 --> 00:05:41,930
The asteroid belt gets stirred into chaos.

99
00:05:41,930 --> 00:05:50,184
And Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, orbiting just beyond a star, would be in real danger of being flung out of the solar system.

100
00:05:50,884 --> 00:05:53,745
But the biggest losers are the closest ones.

101
00:05:53,745 --> 00:05:58,751
Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto have circled Jupiter for billions of years.

102
00:05:58,751 --> 00:06:02,262
Now the thing they orbit becomes eighty times heavier.

103
00:06:02,262 --> 00:06:05,903
What happens to them is the darkest part of this recipe.

104
00:06:06,603 --> 00:06:12,533
If the mass arrived slowly, each moon's orbit would shrink in step, about eighty times smaller.

105
00:06:12,533 --> 00:06:17,776
By our calculation, Io would end up around five thousand kilometers from the center.

106
00:06:17,776 --> 00:06:22,270
Even Callisto, the farthest, would end up at about twenty four thousand.

107
00:06:22,270 --> 00:06:26,140
But the new star is about sixty thousand kilometers in radius.

108
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:28,512
All four moons would end up inside it.

109
00:06:29,212 --> 00:06:32,414
And if the mass arrived all at once, it's no better.

110
00:06:32,414 --> 00:06:41,157
Each moon would suddenly be moving far too slowly for its orbit, and it would fall into a long, narrow path that dives straight into the star.

111
00:06:41,157 --> 00:06:43,743
Either way, Jupiter's moons get swallowed.

112
00:06:43,743 --> 00:06:48,668
Europa's hidden ocean, one of the best places to search for life, would be gone.

113
00:06:49,368 --> 00:06:51,466
Science fiction got here first.

114
00:06:51,466 --> 00:06:52,277
In Arthur C.

115
00:06:52,277 --> 00:07:01,749
Clarke's novel twenty ten, Odyssey Two, Jupiter is ignited into a small new sun, and icy Europa thaws into a warm world where life can grow.

116
00:07:01,749 --> 00:07:03,238
It's a beautiful idea.

117
00:07:03,238 --> 00:07:05,674
But real physics runs the other way.

118
00:07:05,674 --> 00:07:09,733
Make Jupiter heavy enough to shine, and Europa isn't warmed.

119
00:07:09,733 --> 00:07:10,680
It's devoured.

120
00:07:11,380 --> 00:07:12,217
Now the twist.

121
00:07:12,217 --> 00:07:16,220
The Sun is about halfway through its roughly ten billion year life.

122
00:07:16,220 --> 00:07:21,597
A red dwarf this small sips its fuel so slowly that it could shine for trillions of years.

123
00:07:21,597 --> 00:07:29,244
Long after the Sun has swollen up and faded into a cooling white dwarf, the star that used to be Jupiter would still be glowing.

124
00:07:29,244 --> 00:07:32,948
The smallest star in the system would be the last one shining.

125
00:07:33,648 --> 00:07:35,309
So here's the full recipe.

126
00:07:35,309 --> 00:07:38,822
About eighty Jupiters of mass, brought in from outside.

127
00:07:38,822 --> 00:07:42,399
A red point in our sky about as bright as the full Moon.

128
00:07:42,399 --> 00:07:46,232
A wobbling Sun, a stable Earth, and outer planets in danger.

129
00:07:46,232 --> 00:07:48,340
And four famous moons, swallowed.

130
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:51,087
Science fiction has ignited Jupiter before.

131
00:07:51,087 --> 00:07:53,386
Real physics says it stays a planet.

132
00:07:53,386 --> 00:07:55,813
And for Europa, that's very good news.

133
00:07:56,514 --> 00:07:59,478
Next time, we give Earth something only the giants have.

134
00:07:59,478 --> 00:08:01,383
What if Earth had rings like Saturn?

135
00:08:01,383 --> 00:08:05,670
What would they look like from your street, and what would they do to our nights?

136
00:08:05,670 --> 00:08:06,305
That's next.
